# {{title}}

A server of requests and approvals. What people fill in, who approves it and when it is due are in
files under `project/`. The command [ritsu](https://github.com/i2y/ritsu) checks them and makes Go
code from them, and the framework [ringi-server](https://{{framework}}) runs that code as a server:
the pages people use in a browser, and an API.
{{ja_link}}
{{if temporal}}
`ritsu ringi init` wrote these files as a start: a purchase request, an expense claim whose payment
starts once it is approved, a small company, a server, and a worker that runs the payment. They are
yours to change.
{{else}}
`ritsu ringi init` wrote these files as a start: a purchase request, a small company, and a server.
They are yours to change.
{{end}}

## Run it

{{if temporal}}
The payment of an expense claim runs on Temporal. Start its server for development, with the Temporal
CLI ([how to install it](https://docs.temporal.io/cli)):

```sh
temporal server start-dev
```

Then start the worker, which runs the payment, and the server, each in a terminal of its own:

```sh
go mod tidy
go run ./cmd/worker
go run ./cmd/server -dev-login
```
{{else}}
```sh
go mod tidy
go run ./cmd/server -dev-login
```
{{end}}

Open http://127.0.0.1:8080/pages/ in a browser, and choose who you are.

1. As Sales staff 1 (`s1201`), make a purchase request.
2. Sign out, and approve it as the manager of Sales Section 1 (`s1101`).
3. Sign out, and approve it as the head of Sales (`s1001`).
{{if temporal}}
4. As Sales staff 1, make an expense claim, and approve it as the manager of Sales Section 1. The
   worker's log says `paid` and `told`. Nothing is paid: the tasks of `payments.go` only write to the
   log, and you put your company's own work in their place.
{{end}}

The notices are written to the server's log. The requests are in memory, and are gone when the server
stops. `-dev-login` checks no one, so the server answers this computer only.
{{if mcp}}

## Use it from an agent

Agents call the server's tools at http://127.0.0.1:8080/mcp, over MCP's streamable HTTP. With
`-dev-login`, an agent sends the cookie `ringi-dev-person=s1201` (in a browser, the page `/login` sets
it). On a server started without that flag, it sends the header `X-Person: s1201`. What it may do is
what that person may do on the pages. [Let agents make and approve requests]({{site}}agents/) lists
the tools, and says how your staff's agents sign in.
{{end}}

## What is in it

| File | What it is |
|---|---|
| `project/purchase.ringi` | The purchase request: what to fill in, the order of the approvals, the deadlines, and examples of how a request goes |
{{if temporal}}
| `project/expense.ringi` | The expense claim, which starts its payment once it is approved |
{{end}}
| `project/org/2026-04-01/` | The company's teams, people and who belongs where (CSV). The date is the day this data takes effect |
